<header><p class="eyebrow">Depression &amp; Mood Disorders · Mental health in later life</p>
<h1>When "just old age" is actually depression</h1>
<p class="lead">A 74-year-old woman visits her doctor six times in four months — for burning feet, chest heaviness, indigestion and exhaustion. Every test comes back clear. Nobody has asked her how she is feeling, or whether anything still brings her joy. She lost her husband eleven months ago and now spends most days alone in a house that has become too quiet. She does not say "I am sad." She says, "My body is finished." And everyone she sees takes her literally.</p>
<div class="blog-answer"><strong>In short:</strong> Depression in older adults seldom presents as crying or confessed hopelessness. It arrives wearing the costume of the body — pain, weakness, gastric trouble, breathlessness, insomnia — and gets filed under "what happens at this age." Roughly one in five older patients who walk into a clinic with unexplained physical complaints is carrying an undiagnosed depressive illness. It is highly treatable, but only if somebody thinks to look for it.</div>
<div class="article-meta-bar"><time datetime="2026-09-27">27 September 2026</time><span>16 min read</span><span>By Mind Grace Neuropsychiatric Clinic</span></div>
<p>Depression in later life is one of the most missed diagnoses in medicine — not because it is rare, but because it does not look the way we have been taught to expect. This guide covers what late-life depression actually sounds like, why it hides inside physical complaints, what families and clinicians can do about it, and which treatments have real evidence behind them.</p></header>

<nav class="article-toc" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><ol><li><a href="#how-common">How common is it, really?</a></li><li><a href="#what-it-sounds-like">What it actually sounds like</a></li><li><a href="#why-missed">Why it gets missed</a></li><li><a href="#three-rules">Three rules that sort medical from mood</a></li><li><a href="#red-flags">Red flags that mean today, not next month</a></li><li><a href="#asking">How to ask — and what to use</a></li><li><a href="#biology">Why the body hurts when the mind is low</a></li><li><a href="#treatment">What actually helps</a></li><li><a href="#caregivers">For families and caregivers</a></li><li><a href="#faq">Your questions, answered</a></li><li><a href="#next-steps">A closing note</a></li><li><a href="#clinic-help">Getting help at Mind Grace</a></li></ol></nav>

<section id="how-common">
<h2>How common is it, really?</h2>
<p>Late-life depression affects around 1–5% of older adults living independently in the community, but that figure climbs to roughly 11.5% among those receiving home healthcare and up to 35% in institutional care (Reynolds et al., 2022). The more uncomfortable statistic is this: a substantial share of those cases never present as a mood problem at all.</p>
<p>A meta-analysis by Hegeman and colleagues confirmed what geriatric psychiatrists had long suspected at the bedside — older adults with major depression report significantly more hypochondriacal worry, general somatic symptoms and gastrointestinal complaints than younger depressed patients, while reporting <em>less</em> guilt and <em>less</em> overt psychological distress (Hegeman et al., 2015). In plain terms: as we age, depression shifts its vocabulary from the mind to the body.</p>
<p>A 2025 community study put a number on the consequence. Among older adults presenting with somatisation — physical complaints without a matching medical explanation — the incidence of depressive symptoms was 20.37% (Tang et al., 2025). One in five. And the stakes are not abstract: Jeong and colleagues showed that the number and severity of somatic symptoms in community-dwelling elders tracks directly with the severity of depression and with suicide risk (Jeong et al., 2014). Every unexplained ache and every complaint of <em>susti</em> (profound weakness) that goes unexamined is a missed chance to intervene before crisis.</p>
</section>

<section id="what-it-sounds-like">
<h2>What it actually sounds like</h2>
<p>Forget the textbook picture — the weeping patient, the spoken despair, the classic "I can't go on any further." In late life, depression wears a different costume. It speaks in the language of the body. Learning to hear it means collecting the phrases patients actually use, because those phrases repeat across clinics with remarkable consistency.</p>
<p><strong>Pain that moves around.</strong> Diffuse, migrating aches that follow no nerve territory and no X-ray finding: a lower back that hurts far more than the mild wear-and-tear on the scan can explain, a "band" or "weight" across the head, a burning sensation creeping up both soles (<em>paon mein jalan</em>) or across the scalp — blamed on "heat in the body" or "nerve weakness," and stubbornly unmoved by painkillers and nerve medications alike.</p>
<p><strong>The gut that will not cooperate.</strong> Persistent bloating and "gas" (<em>vata</em>, <em>hawa</em>) that ignores antacids and PPIs, morning nausea, and weight loss the family attributes to "he just doesn't feel like eating" — when what has actually gone missing is the pleasure of eating.</p>
<p><strong>Vitality collapse.</strong> <em>Susti</em>: a word with no clean English translation, meaning a profound, unexplained weakness that matters enormously in South Asian geriatric practice. The patient insists they cannot do anything, yet the neurological examination shows muscles that work perfectly well. You may also notice the slowing itself — soft speech, long pauses before answering, fewer gestures, less spontaneous movement.</p>
<p><strong>Sleep that ends too early.</strong> Waking two or three hours before dawn, unable to return to sleep, then lying awake in the dark. Most older patients will not volunteer this unless asked directly; they will frame it as "my body won't let me rest."</p>
<p><strong>Chest and breath.</strong> Air hunger — "I can't take a full breath" — after cardiology has cleared the heart. And <em>dil doobna</em>, the "sinking heart": a visceral wave of dread in the chest with no tachycardia, no pain and a normal ECG.</p>
<p><strong>Preoccupation with the body.</strong> Repeated requests for another test, another scan, another opinion, even though every result has been normal. This is not attention-seeking and it is not malingering. It is genuine, distressing amplification of bodily signals driven by the depressive state.</p>
<p>Under all of this sits a cultural logic worth naming plainly. In many Indian families, saying "I am depressed" carries stigma; saying "my stomach is giving trouble" does not. When emotional pain cannot be voiced safely, the body becomes the socially acceptable vessel for it. Historically this was called "masked depression"; Prisco and colleagues trace how the idea matured into today's understanding of somatic symptom presentations within DSM-5-TR (Prisco et al., 2023). Clinicians and families must resist reading these idioms of distress as exaggeration. </p>
<p>Two distinctions are worth committing to memory. First, somatic depression versus <strong>somatic symptom disorder</strong>: in the former, treating the depression turns the bodily volume down; in the latter, the preoccupation persists even when mood lifts, and therapy targets the illness belief itself rather than mood. Second, late-life depression versus <strong>generalised anxiety</strong>, which in this generation often wears the identical bodily costume — the differentiator is what dominates the quiet hours: recurrent worry about future events points to anxiety, while anhedonia, early-morning waking and a settled hopelessness point to depression. Both can coexist, and both respond to overlapping treatments — which is precisely why the underlying mood state must be named.</p>
</section>

<section id="why-missed">
<h2>Why it gets missed</h2>
<p>The core difficulty is what Drayer and colleagues described as a genuine diagnostic dilemma: the somatic symptoms of depression — fatigue, disturbed sleep, appetite change, slowed thinking and movement — are almost indistinguishable from the symptoms of the chronic medical conditions that accompany ageing (Drayer et al., 2005).</p>
<div class="article-tables"><table class="article-table">
<caption>The same complaint, two possible explanations — why a single symptom proves nothing.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">The complaint</th><th scope="col">Could be depression</th><th scope="col">Could be medical illness</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Fatigue</td><td>Psychomotor retardation, loss of interest</td><td>Heart failure, hypothyroidism, anaemia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weight loss</td><td>Appetite change, anhedonia</td><td>Malignancy, hyperthyroidism, malabsorption</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insomnia</td><td>Hyperarousal, early-morning waking</td><td>Sleep apnoea, nocturia, chronic pain</td></tr>
<tr><td>Slowed thinking</td><td>Depressive pseudodementia</td><td>Early Alzheimer's disease, vascular dementia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pain</td><td>Central sensitisation, lowered pain threshold</td><td>Osteoarthritis, neuropathy, spinal stenosis</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>This overlap invites two opposite errors, and both are harmful. <strong>Diagnostic overshadowing</strong> attributes every new complaint to the illnesses already on the problem list, so the depression is never named. <strong>Over-attribution the other way</strong> labels whatever cannot be explained as "psychological," and abandons a legitimate medical workup. A good clinician — or an attentive son or daughter — holds both possibilities open until the pattern resolves.</p>
</section>

<section id="three-rules">
<h2>Three rules that sort medical from mood</h2>
<p>Rather than agonising over individual symptoms, look at the shape of the story. Three sequential checks settle most real-world cases.</p>
<p><strong>Rule 1 — Look at the timeline.</strong> If a physical symptom clearly preceded any change in mood or daily functioning by more than six months, a medical cause deserves priority. If several <em>new</em> complaints appeared together within a few weeks of an identifiable loss — a bereavement, a relocation, a child moving abroad, a forced retirement — suspicion should tilt toward depression. A cluster of simultaneous new symptoms is almost never one disease.</p>
<p><strong>Rule 2 — Notice treatment resistance.</strong> When a specific complaint has failed two adequate, properly dosed courses of standard medical treatment, stop adding the same layer again. Back pain that has survived eight weeks of anti-inflammatories and eight weeks of physiotherapy, with only mild changes on imaging, is very likely being amplified by a depressive process — and will respond to treating that instead.</p>
<p><strong>Rule 3 — Compare the map to the territory.</strong> Ask the patient to rate their suffering and their functional impairment out of ten, then rate the objective findings yourself out of ten. A gap of four points or more between subjective distress and measurable pathology is a signature of somatic depression. The suffering is real; the body is simply turned up louder than the damage.</p>
</section>

<section id="red-flags">
<h2>Red flags that mean today, not next month</h2>
<p>Somatic depression is usually a clinic conversation, but sometimes it is an emergency. Older adults rarely say "I want to die" — they say it with their behaviour. Call a psychiatrist or seek same-day help if any of these appear: talking or writing about death, giving away treasured possessions, sudden unexplained "peacefulness" after weeks of complaint (a well-documented warning sign that a decision has been made), refusing food or fluids to the point of weight loss and dehydration, or a first episode of depression-like symptoms after age 65 with no clear stressor — which always warrants ruling out medical causes such as hypothyroidism, vitamin B12 deficiency, and early neurocognitive disorder before assuming a psychiatric label.</p>
<p>One more rule deserves emphasis because families hear only reassurance from rushed consultations: in later life, despair is never a normal variant of ageing. "What do you expect at her age?" is not a diagnosis. Persistent low mood, withdrawal or unexplained physical suffering that lasts two weeks deserves a proper assessment — the same urgency you would apply to chest pain.</p>
</section>

<section id="asking">
<h2>How to ask — and what to use</h2>
<p>The single highest-yield intervention costs nothing: ask. Not "are you depressed?" — which many older patients reject on principle — but the questions underneath it. Are you sleeping? Is food tasting of anything? When did you last enjoy something? What has changed since <em>he</em> passed / since <em>she</em> left for Canada? Who do you talk to all day?</p>
<p>Standard screening instruments trip over the same obstacle as the clinicians who use them. The widely used PHQ-9 contains four somatic items out of nine; in a 78-year-old with heart failure and osteoarthritis those items score positive regardless of psychiatric state, generating false alarms. A tiered approach works better:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Geriatric Depression Scale (GDS-15)</strong> for primary screening — deliberately built without somatic items, focusing on the cognitive and emotional core ("Do you feel that your life is empty?"). A score of 5 or above indicates probable depression, and it takes three minutes.</li>
<li><strong>PHQ-15</strong> to quantify the bodily-symptom burden separately — not to diagnose depression, but to measure how much of the presentation is somatic. High on both scales is strong convergent evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Cornell Scale for Depression in Dementia (CSDD)</strong> when memory is failing and self-report becomes unreliable. It is clinician-administered with caregiver input and captures non-verbal distress — facial tension, frequent sighing, physical restlessness, mood that is worse in the morning. A score of 8 or above suggests depression.</li>
<li><strong>The family interview</strong>, which no scale replaces. Relatives notice what patients minimise: abandoned routines, neglected hygiene, uncharacteristic irritability, refusals to eat.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are a family member reading this rather than a doctor: none of these instruments requires a prescription, but interpretation does. Use them to start an informed conversation with a psychiatrist or geriatric specialist, not to self-diagnose.</p>
</section>

<section id="biology">
<h2>Why the body hurts when the mind is low</h2>
<p>Somatic depression is not "all in the head" — and saying so would be scientifically wrong. Three lines of evidence show the body and mind are entangled here in measurable ways.</p>
<p><strong>Symptoms form one network.</strong> Triolo and colleagues applied network analysis to older adults and found that chronic somatic disease and depressive symptoms are structurally interconnected within a single system, with fatigue, pain and sleep disturbance acting as "bridge nodes" through which medical illness activates the depressive network (Triolo et al., 2021). The tiredness produced by diabetes is not a side character in the depression; it is often the mechanism of it.</p>
<p>This is the loop Mrs. Kapoor has been living inside for eleven months: gastritis that suppresses appetite, appetite loss that deepens fatigue, fatigue that ends the evening walk, and a body whose every unexplained signal invites another normal report — each clearance tightening the net rather than loosening it.</p>
<p><strong>Inflammation mimics sadness.</strong> Chronic low-grade inflammation of ageing ("inflammaging") raises cytokines such as IL-6, TNF-α and CRP, which cross into the brain and produce the classic sickness behaviour — fatigue, loss of pleasure, withdrawal, heightened pain sensitivity. Depressed older adults are, quite literally, behaving like people fighting an infection.</p>
<p><strong>Vessels shape mood.</strong> Cerebrovascular disease, especially small-vessel change in the frontal-subcortical circuits, disrupts the wiring that regulates motivation and emotion. "Vascular depression" has a recognisable face: prominent apathy, slowed movement, many somatic complaints — and comparatively little visible sadness.</p>
</section>

<section id="treatment">
<h2>What actually helps</h2>
<p>Treatment aims at both ends of the loop at once: lift the mood, and reduce the bodily burden that is driving the functional decline. The good news is that late-life depression responds well — often better than people expect — when treated deliberately. Expect the antidepressant to need four to six weeks before the mood shifts, and up to eight before energy and sleep fully recover; start low, go slow, and review regularly rather than abandon ship at the first side effect. Remission rates in this age group are genuinely high, and roughly eighty percent of older adults who achieve remission stay well over the following year. Relapse prevention is not an afterthought: once a second episode has occurred, continuing treatment for at least two years — sometimes indefinitely — is what keeps Mrs. Kapoor out of the revolving door of specialists.</p>
<h3>Medication chosen for the symptom profile</h3>
<p>There is no single best antidepressant for older adults; there is a best fit for the presentation in front of you. Prescribing decisions belong with a psychiatrist, but it helps families to understand the reasoning.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>When pain dominates:</strong> duloxetine, an SNRI started at 30 mg and taken forward to 60 mg, provides antidepressant and pain-relieving action through the same descending pain pathways. Sodium levels and blood pressure need monitoring, and tricyclic antidepressants are generally avoided in this age group because of anticholinergic effects and fall risk (Beers Criteria).</li>
<li><strong>When insomnia and weight loss dominate:</strong> mirtazapine at 7.5–15 mg at night. Curiously, the lower doses are more sedating; its appetite stimulation and sleep restoration make it well suited to the frail, under-eating older adult.</li>
<li><strong>When apathy dominates and there is already a handful of medicines:</strong> escitalopram 5 mg rising to 10 mg, chosen for the cleanest drug-interaction profile among the SSRIs.</li>
<li><strong>When nothing has worked:</strong> ECT remains the most effective — and most under-used — treatment for severe, treatment-resistant depression in the elderly, including the somatic presentations. Modern right-unilateral ultrabrief-pulse techniques are far safer than their reputation suggests and often clear mood and body symptoms together (Devita et al., 2022).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Therapy that starts where the patient is</h3>
<p>Standard talk therapy frequently fails here for one reason: the patient's belief that this is a physical problem is partly correct, and being told otherwise feels like being called a liar. Effective therapy begins with validation — "I believe your pain is real. I am not saying it is in your head. I am saying there is a treatment that can turn the volume down on both the pain and the heaviness." From there, problem-solving therapy breaks the monolithic "everything hurts" into discrete, manageable pieces, and behavioural activation scaled radically down to what the body can do — sit in the courtyard for ten minutes, fold laundry while seated, play the evening <em>bhajan</em> — with symptoms rated before and after, so the patient gathers personal evidence that movement reduces distress.</p>
<p>The strongest structural evidence belongs to collaborative care. The IMPACT trial showed that integrating a depression care manager with primary care and consulting psychiatry produced markedly better outcomes for both mood and physical functioning than usual care alone (Unützer et al., 2002). One person needs to hold the whole plan; fragmented specialists each clearing one organ is precisely how Mrs. Kapoor fell through six appointments.</p>
<h3>The polypharmacy trap</h3>
<p>Before adding anything, subtract. Older adults with somatic complaints accumulate prescriptions — NSAIDs, acid suppressants, opioids, and worst of all benzodiazepines for "nerves" and sleep, which worsen cognition, multiply falls and can create a dysphoric state that mimics the depression it was meant to soothe. Judicious, supervised deprescribing is often the first therapeutic step in somatic depression.</p>
<h3>The physical side of the loop</h3>
<p>Treating the mood does not excuse abandoning the body — and this is where many guides go wrong. Depression and its comorbidities feed each other bidirectionally: pain disrupts sleep, poor sleep lowers the pain threshold and mood, immobility breeds deconditioning, and deconditioning reads as more “weakness.” So alongside the antidepressant, three unglamorous interventions carry real evidence: structured, graded exercise adapted to capacity (even supervised walking programmes measurably reduce late-life depressive symptoms), treatment of sleep apnoea when snoring and unrefreshed mornings point to it, and correction of the reversible metabolic contributors that masquerade as depression in this age group — thyroid dysfunction, vitamin B12 and vitamin D deficiency, anaemia, and poorly controlled diabetes. Get these baseline bloods before, not after, concluding that everything is psychiatric.</p>
</section>

<section id="caregivers">
<h2>For families and caregivers</h2>
<p>You know your parent better than any fifteen-minute consultation allows. Trust the pattern you live with. Specific things to watch for, beyond the complaints themselves: a sudden proliferation of new symptoms at once; tests ordered repeatedly with results coming back normal; early-morning waking followed by hours sitting in the dark; abandoned prayers, walks, grandchildren's visits, favourite programmes; food described as tasteless; hygiene slipping; "what's the point of me now" said as casually as a comment about the weather; and grief that was never processed because the family, busy with their own lives, never sat and let them talk about the person who died.</p>
<p>Two cautions. Do not argue with the physical complaint — "all the reports are fine, so there is nothing wrong" confirms the patient's fear that nobody believes them, and drives the somatic expression harder. And take any mention of being a burden, or of wanting to "finish," seriously; in older adults, depression carries a real suicide risk, and men over 60 are among the highest-risk groups.</p>
<p>What to say instead is simpler than families expect. Sit beside them and ask about the person underneath the complaints: <em>“Ma, what was it like, looking after everyone for forty years and then having all this quiet?”</em> Ask permission before suggesting help — <em>“Would you be open to seeing someone who treats the whole body, not just the stomach?”</em> — and frame the psychiatrist the way you would frame a cardiologist: a specialist for a specific, common, treatable illness of later life, not an admission that the mind has “failed.” Then do the logistical things only you can do: book the appointment, accompany them to it, sit in the waiting room, and carry the medication calendar home. Adherence in this age group is won or lost in the family, not the pharmacy.</p>
</section>

<section id="next-steps">
<h2>A closing note</h2>
<p>The next time an older person tells you their body is falling apart — that nothing works, that they are too weak to walk to the temple at the end of the street — resist the reflex to order one more scan or add one more analgesic. Pause, and ask about mood, sleep, appetite, pleasure, loss and loneliness. Three minutes with the right questions can surface a treatable illness that six specialists missed.</p>
<p>Late-life depression hiding behind physical complaints is not a diagnostic curiosity. It is common, it is dangerous, and it is treatable. The only thing standing between a patient and recovery is someone who knows what to look for — and there is no reason to wait for the next six-appointment carousel to start again. If an older person you love has been chasing unexplained symptoms for weeks, book a geriatric-focused assessment now. Bring every report and prescription from every previous visit; a fresh set of eyes on the whole picture, in one place, is exactly what was missing.</p>
</section>

