<section id="faq">
<h2>Your questions, answered</h2>
<p><strong>Is this just how ageing feels?</strong> No. Aching joints and slower healing are part of ageing; a two-week stretch of lost interest, early-morning waking, and despair dressed as physical complaint is not. Ageing narrows the body's reserve, but depression steals its will — and only one of those two is treatable with an antidepressant.</p>
<p><strong>Will medication make my parent dependent or “drugged”?</strong> Modern first-line antidepressants used at geriatric doses are not addictive and do not sedate personality. The genuinely risky drugs in this population are the ones already sitting in the cupboard — benzodiazepines and opioids — which is why treatment usually involves subtraction as well as addition.</p>
<p><strong>My parent refuses to see a psychiatrist. What now?</strong> Start where they will meet you: the physician who already treats their body. Ask for a combined evaluation, or frame the appointment as a “nerve and sleep” clinic visit rather than a mental-health visit. Teleconsultation works remarkably well for home-bound elders. Never argue the diagnosis; invite the assessment.</p>
<p><strong>How long until we see a change?</strong> Sleep and appetite often shift within two weeks; mood and pain take four to eight. Judge the medicine at six weeks, not ten days, and never stop it abruptly because things improved — that is how relapse arrives.</p>
<p><strong>Could this be a physical illness instead of depression?</strong> Possibly — and that is exactly why proper assessment includes baseline blood work. Thyroid dysfunction, vitamin B12 and D deficiency, anaemia, uncontrolled diabetes and early neurocognitive disorders can all masquerade as late-life depression. A first-ever depressive episode after 65 with no clear trigger should be medically worked up before any psychiatric label is applied. Good treatment begins by ruling out the body, then treating both.</p>
<p><strong>Can exercise or family attention replace treatment?</strong> They are powerful allies — walking programmes, restructured routines and engaged grandchildren measurably help mild cases — but for moderate to severe depression they work <em>with</em> medication and structured therapy, not instead of them. Love is not a substitute for treatment; it is the delivery mechanism for it.</p>
</section>
